EXT. SKYLINE ROOFTOP BAR - NIGHT

Detective Annika Brandt, 40s, sits at the far end of the bar with a club soda and a view of the elevator. She keeps her jacket on. Under it, a wire taped below the ribs. Her phone sits face up beside the glass.

The phone BUZZES. She lets it ring twice, then answers and puts it to her ear.

HALE (V.O.)
Tell me you're up there.

BRANDT
Corner. By the heater. Where are you?

HALE (V.O.)
Parking structure. Level three. I'm giving it five minutes.

BRANDT
Five minutes is fine.

HALE (V.O.)
You come alone?

BRANDT
You asked me to.

The line goes quiet. Wind snaps a string of lights overhead. She watches the elevator numbers climb on the display, 2, 3, 4, and her thumb goes still on the glass.

BRANDT (CONT'D)
Hale. Level three. You said that already?

HALE (V.O.)
Yeah. Why?

BRANDT
No reason. Come on up.

She ends the call. The phone lights.

ON PHONE: unknown number -- "ur not alone up there"

She reads it once. She does not look around. She sets the phone face down and slides a hand into her jacket, thumbs the transmitter.

BRANDT (CONT'D)
(low, to the wire)
Unit two, confirm the garage camera. Level three. Is anyone there?

A beat. Nothing in the earpiece. Static, then a CLICK.

The elevator DINGS. Tomasz Hale, 50s, rumpled suit, steps out and scans the roof. He sees her, hesitates, then crosses to the stool beside hers. He sits too carefully, as though the cushion might be wired.

HALE
You look like a cop.

BRANDT
You look like a man who's late.

HALE
I wasn't in the garage.

She turns her head.

BRANDT
Say that again.

HALE
I took the stairs. I was on the stairs the whole time. The call, it wasn't me calling.

Her phone BUZZES against the bar. She doesn't touch it. He nods at it.

HALE (CONT'D)
That's him. He's got my phone. He's got my kid's school schedule too, Detective, that was the part I couldn't, I had to come.

BRANDT
Who has your phone?

HALE
The man at the table behind you. Blue shirt. Don't turn around.

The ice in her glass shifts. Somewhere behind her a chair scrapes.

ON PHONE: unknown number -- "good. now both of u stand up"

BRANDT
(barely moving)
How long has he been sitting there?

HALE
Since before I got here. He walked me up.

BRANDT
And the garage?

HALE
There's no one in the garage. There's never been anyone. He wanted you to say it out loud.

She exhales. Her hand comes out of the jacket empty. Behind her, a glass is set down, softly, and a shadow lengthens across the bar top.

BRANDT
Then he's already heard everything.

HALE
Every word.

The phone BUZZES a third time. Neither of them looks at it. She slides off the stool, standing straight, still not turning around. He stays seated, staring into his lap.

BRANDT
(quietly)
Stay seated, Tomasz. Whatever happens next, you stay in that chair.

HALE
What are you going to do?

She lifts the wire's transmitter out from under her shirt in plain view and lays it on the bar.

BRANDT
Say hello.

She turns around.
